A||||8||zeusrekning||headliner question||||1||
Z||000000||zeusrekning||09-12-2000||06:49 AM||||can you make a headliner with no third brakelight fit into a car with a third brakelight?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||09-12-2000||09:13 AM||||Yes. Very simple to do. I just used a dremel to cut out the spot for the brake light. Then recover as usual.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HugGRRRFiero||09-12-2000||09:30 AM||||Sure can, you just can't use a headliner from a sunroof car in one with a hardtop.  I'd measure everything out with a pencil before cutting anything.<P>If your looking to save from re-doing the headliner, then try to cut the rectangle out of just the cardboard material and cut a small X in the liner where the cutout is.  If you don't have a third brake light headliner as a template, then you will need to put the headliner in and make sure you have clearance to the third brake light.  Make sure you leave enough board material so the plastic cover plate catches the edges of the cut out area.  This might take some trial and error and eyeballing.<P>Now, finish cutting X shape in the headliner material and wrap it around the opening and glue everything back down on the backside with 3M spray adhesive.<P>To make things simpler you could probably just cut a rectangle out of both the board and liner material.  Folding the edges of the liner over is an extra step but will help prevent the liner from wanting to pull back from the edges.  Good luck, hope this helps give you ideas...<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||whamel||09-12-2000||10:24 AM||||     I'm guessing this is just due to the hole in the roof, right?  I have a unique situation that my roof has been replaced, it's a sunroof car with a solid roof now - and I also have no headliner.  So I guess I had better ask before I get started.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>Thanks,<BR>Wade<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by HugGRRRFiero:<BR><B>Sure can, you just can't use a headliner from a sunroof car in one with a hardtop.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||HugGRRRFiero||09-12-2000||10:55 AM||||Ok, I should have put the  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> smiley guy next to that quote, Geez that was just my initial reaction to the question.  Sorry, I hope that I had offered some good advice here.<P>I apologize for that sarcasm, Thanks for pointing that out.||||reg||9
Z||000005||lawrence||09-12-2000||12:29 PM||||Do you really need a Dremel to cut it?  The headliner in my 88 is a very soft material.  Looks like fiberglass insulation pressed into a mold.  A scissors should be enough to cut it.<P>If you do use a Dremel, make sure you wear a mask.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000006||pollock||09-12-2000||02:37 PM||||You need to rough-out the cut with a plasma cutter, and then take it to a CNC machine shop to have them finish the hole out to factory specs.  You can download the dimensions from Design One for just $3999.99.<P>Or use scissors.  I wouldn't bother with the dremel.||||reg||1
Z||000007||zeusrekning||09-12-2000||04:50 PM||||Thanks. about the plasma cutter thing. I was using one at school a few weeks before i graduated. (school plasma cutter tips get cloged quick)Iwas wiping the tip off with the palm off my hand(wearing welding gloves) and leaning on the grounded table when the thick glove pushed the trigger in shot a hole halfway through my palm. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||09-12-2000||11:03 PM||||Smooth the rough edges around the hole in your hand using a palm sander.  Makes it easier to get gloves on and off.||||reg||12
